 ## Overview
-Histogram Noise Reduction is a preprocessing step applied to the 3D histogram of a volume dataset to mitigate noise in underrepresented regions where voxel counts are too low for valid classification [S1]. It solves the problem of sparse data causing invalid opacity assignments by artificially increasing sample density through super-sampling and neighborhood expansion [S1]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Histogram Equalization](5642-histogram-equalization.md) by focusing on statistical smoothing of the histogram bin counts rather than redistributing intensity values, and from [Boundary Emphasis](12217-boundary-emphasis.md) by prioritizing the removal of noise over the enhancement of specific features [G].
+Histogram Noise Reduction is a preprocessing step applied to the 3D histogram of a volume dataset to mitigate noise in underrepresented regions where voxel counts are too low for valid classification [S1]. It solves the problem of sparse data causing invalid opacity assignments by artificially increasing sample density through super-sampling and neighborhood expansion [S1]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Histogram Equalization](5642-histogram-equalization.md) by focusing on statistical smoothing of the histogram bin counts rather than redistributing intensity values [G]. It differs from [Boundary Emphasis](12217-boundary-emphasis.md) by prioritizing the removal of noise over the enhancement of specific features [G].
 
